ref 17592186546722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546869 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546869 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546869 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546869 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546869 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6768b380-738a-5723-a58c-cf6e6b14e43c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546912 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab119a6d-ecae-5c97-9ab8-0bc9b74391d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546926 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "be292a4e-e0bf-5c61-a277-07e6b41b4f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00d15146-6fbb-5275-8798-98c048c587e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546958 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+y=0 \\\\ -6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(-3x+y)=-6x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33c6aa75-206a-500b-acd6-afa11d420835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546987 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fde17e6b52cb472d517caee4fcb84e9aee9465ebd391f482316b487486ebe6e0.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/5d/5d95c4d8ab87366960b99dbbcd898214e2ee883da7ceb1926d44968c204d2497.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ce7a74d-231a-58e8-b9b6-91d4b9871cc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547001 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/033fb4b6f35c06e8d718df9b8e88d47b7461f247121f1e859c6fba41cfda9112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/ea/eabc6882089c4e26e497b8adae004018e132874dd9e02d33caeddd3967330c87.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/868becabde44fa1173fcf0a41424bfcad678a03f5449f62706a7f814563347dc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b08d2a40-8148-5020-a105-36a37de12f17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547042 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x+6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n3(x+2y)=3x+6y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,x=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
